{"step_index":28,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T01:13:16Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-10T06:43:16+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-10T06:43:21+05:30\nThe search for \"Qwen 3 4B 8B 14B 32B VRAM GGUF Q4_K_M benchmark tokens per second 2025\" returned the following summary:\nThe Qwen3 model series, released in April 2025, includes a range of dense and Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models. Below is the information regarding their VRAM requirements and performance expectations for GGUF Q4_K_M quantization.\n\n### VRAM Requirements (GGUF Q4_K_M)\nMemory usage is primarily determined by the number of parameters and the size of the KV cache (which grows with context length). For Q4_K_M quantization, estimated VRAM requirements for the dense models are as follows:\n\n| Model Size | Approx. Weight VRAM (Q4_K_M) | Recommended Total VRAM (inc. Context) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Qwen3-4B** | ~2.5 GB | 4–6 GB |\n| **Qwen3-8B** | ~5.0 GB | 8–10 GB |\n| **Qwen3-14B** | ~9.0 GB | 12–16 GB |\n| **Qwen3-32B** | ~19.5 GB | 24–32 GB |\n\n*Note: These are estimates. Larger context windows (e.g., 32K or 128K) will significantly increase the KV cache VRAM requirement beyond these base figures.*\n\n### Tokens Per Second (TPS) Benchmarks\nTokens per second are heavily dependent on your specific hardware (memory bandwidth is the primary bottleneck for inference speed). As of 2025–2026, general performance trends for Q4_K_M models on consumer hardware include:\n\n*   **Memory Bandwidth Bound:** Inference speed is typically limited by how fast your hardware can move weights from VRAM to the compute units. Consequently, high-end GPUs (e.g., RTX 4090/5090) or unified memory systems (Apple Silicon) achieve significantly higher speeds.\n*   **Performance Ranges:**\n    *   **High-end GPUs (e.g., RTX 5090):** Typically achieve **60–100+ t/s** for mid-sized models (8B–32B) at Q4_K_M.\n    *   **Mid-range/Laptop GPUs:** Often see **20–50 t/s**, depending on the model size and thermal headroom.\n    *   **Unified Memory (Apple M-series):** Performance is highly competitive, often ranging from **30–70 t/s** depending on the specific chip (Max/Ultra) and model size.\n\n### Key Considerations for 2025/2026\n*   **Model Existence:** Qwen3 models (0.6B, 1.7B, 4B, 8B, 14B, 32B, and MoE variants like 30B-A3B and 235B-A22B) are officially released and widely available on platforms like Hugging Face.\n*   **Optimization:** For best results, use `llama.cpp` or engines like `vLLM` or `Ollama`, which support GGUF quantization. If you encounter memory bottlenecks, you can reduce your context window size to free up VRAM, which often improves tokens per second by preventing offloading to system RAM.\n*   **Hardware:** Because these models are memory-bandwidth bound, prioritize GPUs with higher memory bus speeds (e.g., GDDR6X) or integrated systems with high-bandwidth memory (HBM/Unified Memory) if you are targeting consistent high-speed throughput."}
{"step_index":29,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T01:13:17Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-10T06:43:17+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-10T06:43:26+05:30\nThe search for \"embedding model VRAM requirements local 2025 nomic-embed all-MiniLM RAG pipeline\" returned the following summary:\nFor a local RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) pipeline in 2025, embedding models like `nomic-embed-text` and `all-MiniLM` are highly efficient and have very modest VRAM requirements compared to the generative LLMs (e.g., Llama 3, Qwen 3) that typically accompany them in the stack.\n\n### VRAM Requirements Summary\n\n| Model | Estimated VRAM Usage | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`nomic-embed-text`** | **~0.3 GB – 0.5 GB** | Extremely efficient; standard for Ollama-based local RAG. |\n| **`all-MiniLM-L6-v2`** | **~0.1 GB – 0.3 GB** | Very lightweight; can often run on CPU-only systems. |\n\n---\n\n### Key Considerations for Your RAG Pipeline\n\n*   **Embedding vs. Generation:** In a local RAG stack, the \"bottleneck\" is almost always the generative LLM (e.g., an 8B parameter model), not the embedding model. A typical 8B model at 4-bit quantization (Q4_K_M) requires ~6 GB of VRAM. Because embedding models use a fraction of this, they can comfortably sit alongside your primary model on most consumer GPUs (8 GB+).\n*   **Performance vs. Precision:** \n    *   `nomic-embed-text` (137M parameters) is widely considered the best-in-class for local RAG via Ollama due to its strong performance and 8,192-token context window.\n    *   `all-MiniLM-L6-v2` is excellent if your hardware is severely constrained (e.g., legacy systems or devices without dedicated GPUs), as it is one of the lightest models available.\n*   **Implementation Tips:**\n    *   **Prefixes:** For `nomic-embed-text`, ensure you use task prefixes (e.g., `search_document:` for indexing and `search_query:` for retrieval). Omitting these will significantly degrade retrieval quality.\n    *   **Context Window:** When using `nomic-embed-text` via Ollama, the default context might be set to 2,048 tokens. You can manually increase this to its native 8,192 tokens by setting `num_ctx` in your API request options.\n    *   **Colocation:** To minimize latency, run both your embedding model and your LLM on the same local instance (e.g., Ollama). This avoids unnecessary network round-trips, which are often the primary cause of slowness in local RAG pipelines.\n    *   **Memory Overhead:** Remember that while the model weights themselves are small (e.g., ~274 MB for `nomic-embed-text`), frameworks like PyTorch or Ollama have an inherent \"base\" memory overhead (often 0.5 GB – 1 GB) when they initialize CUDA or model loaders. Even so, these models will rarely cause an Out-of-Memory (OOM) error on modern systems.\n\n### Recommendation for 2025\nIf you are building a new RAG pipeline, **`nomic-embed-text`** is the recommended baseline. Its balance of performance, support for long contexts (8k), and massive adoption makes it the most robust choice for offline, private document search."}
