{"step_index":31,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T01:13:32Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-10T06:43:32+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-10T06:43:38+05:30\nThe search for \"Apple Silicon Mac M4 Pro Max Ultra LLM inference VRAM unified memory comparison 2025\" returned the following summary:\nApple Silicon’s **Unified Memory Architecture (UMA)** is the defining feature that makes Mac hardware highly effective for Large Language Model (LLM) inference. Unlike traditional PC architectures that separate CPU RAM from discrete GPU VRAM, Apple Silicon provides a single, high-bandwidth memory pool accessible to the CPU, GPU, and Neural Engine.\n\n### Unified Memory & LLM Inference\n*   **No \"Copy Tax\":** In discrete GPU systems, model weights must be moved across the PCIe bus into VRAM, creating a bottleneck. Apple Silicon eliminates this; the memory is already local to the compute units, allowing for significantly more efficient access to large parameter sets.\n*   **Capacity Advantage:** The VRAM ceiling of consumer GPUs (typically 24GB for an RTX 4090) often prevents the loading of larger models. Apple’s Unified Memory allows for configurations up to 128GB (Max) or even higher (Ultra), enabling the local execution of massive models (e.g., 70B+ parameters) that would otherwise require multi-GPU server setups.\n*   **Bandwidth as the Primary Driver:** LLM inference (specifically the decode phase) is primarily **memory-bandwidth bound**. The speed at which tokens are generated is directly proportional to how fast model weights can be streamed from memory to the GPU cores.\n\n### M4 Lineup Comparison (Focus on LLM Performance)\n\n| Chip | Max Unified Memory | Memory Bandwidth | LLM Capability Note |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **M4** | 32 GB | 120 GB/s | Entry-level; great for 7B–13B models. |\n| **M4 Pro** | 48 GB | 273 GB/s | Solid for mid-size models (up to ~33B). |\n| **M4 Max** | 128 GB | 546 GB/s | Excellent for 70B+ models; high token throughput. |\n\n*Note: While \"Ultra\" chips are part of the broader Apple Silicon history, the M4 generation's performance is driven by these specific bandwidth and capacity tiers. Higher-tier chips like the Max and Ultra provide significantly higher bandwidth, which translates to faster tokens-per-second.*\n\n### Key Considerations for 2025/2026\n*   **Quantization:** Because memory is the primary constraint, most users run quantized models (e.g., Q4_K_M). A model roughly requires 0.6 GB of memory per billion parameters at 4-bit quantization.\n*   **Software Ecosystem:** **MLX** (Apple’s native machine learning framework) has become the gold standard for Apple Silicon, frequently outperforming `llama.cpp` (Metal backend) in both speed and prompt processing.\n*   **Choosing the Right Hardware:**\n    *   **For 7B–13B models:** The base M4 (16GB/24GB/32GB) is highly capable.\n    *   **For 33B models:** The M4 Pro (48GB) is widely considered the \"sweet spot\" for performance per dollar.\n    *   **For 70B+ models:** The M4 Max (64GB/128GB) is necessary to keep the entire model in memory without relying on slow disk swapping.\n\n**Verdict:** While NVIDIA GPUs often offer higher absolute speed (tokens per second) for smaller models due to raw compute power, Apple Silicon is the preferred choice for running **larger, more capable models** locally, thanks to its high memory capacity and unified architecture which removes the traditional VRAM ceiling."}
{"step_index":32,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T01:13:33Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-10T06:43:33+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-10T06:43:43+05:30\nThe search for \"\"small language model\" SLM 1B 3B 4B good enough local AI 2025 phi gemma qwen\" returned the following summary:\nIn 2026, **Small Language Models (SLMs) in the 1B–4B parameter range are widely considered \"good enough\" for many real-world local AI applications**, provided they are matched to the right task.\n\nThe industry has moved beyond equating \"bigger\" with \"smarter.\" Thanks to advancements in high-quality synthetic training data, distillation techniques, and optimized architectures (such as Mixture-of-Experts and E4B-style efficiency), these models now perform tasks that previously required much larger, resource-heavy models.\n\n### Why 1B–4B Models Are Effective in 2025–2026\n*   **Efficiency:** They deliver ultra-low latency, making them ideal for real-time applications like local voice assistants, chatbots, and on-device processing.\n*   **Privacy & Cost:** Because they can run entirely on consumer hardware (laptops, mobile devices, and edge hardware) with 4–8 GB of RAM, they eliminate the need for cloud APIs, ensuring zero cost per inference and complete data privacy.\n*   **Focused Mastery:** Unlike generalist LLMs, these models are often highly effective at targeted tasks like structured data extraction (JSON), summarization, multilingual translation, and simple coding assistance.\n\n### Top Models to Consider\nRecent benchmarks and community feedback highlight several standouts in the sub-4B category:\n\n| Model Family | Best For | Notable Strengths |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Phi-4 Mini (3.8B)** | Reasoning & Logic | Leads its class in reasoning benchmarks (math/logic) and is highly efficient for CPU-only inference. |\n| **Gemma 4 E2B/E4B** | Edge & Multimodality | Excellent for multimodal tasks (text/image/audio) and features a massive 128K context window. |\n| **Qwen2.5 / Qwen3 (1.5B–4B)** | Coding & Agents | Widely praised for coding capabilities, tool-calling, and strong multilingual support. |\n| **Llama 3.2 (1B & 3B)** | General Use | Highly versatile with broad community support; the 3B version is a go-to for general-purpose local applications. |\n\n### Practical Guidance for Local AI\n*   **The \"3B+\" Rule:** For professional or production-grade tasks, community consensus suggests that models in the **3B+ range** (or architectures like Gemma 4 E2B) provide the best balance of quality and reliability. Models under 1B are often best suited for very specific, narrow tasks (e.g., speech classification or simple text cleaning).\n*   **Context Matters:** Many 2026-era SLMs now support **128K context windows**, allowing you to feed them long documents or extensive chat histories, which significantly improves their performance in RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) pipelines.\n*   **Deployment:** You can easily run these using tools like **Ollama, LM Studio, or MLX** (for macOS). These tools handle quantization—a technique that compresses the model to fit into your device’s memory without sacrificing significant intelligence.\n\n**Verdict:** If your goal is to build a local, private, and fast AI application, you do not need a massive 70B+ parameter model. A well-chosen 3B–4B model will likely handle your requirements for reasoning, coding, or assistant tasks while running smoothly on the hardware you already own."}
{"step_index":33,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T01:13:43Z","content":"I now have comprehensive data covering all aspects of the sub-query. Let me compile and send the findings back to the parent agent.","tool_calls":[{"name":"send_message","args":{"Message":"## Sub-query: What are the actual VRAM requirements of current open-source models in 2025-2026?\n\n---\n\n## Key Findings\n\n### 1. LLM VRAM Requirements (Full Precision & Quantized)\n\nThe universal formula: **VRAM ≈ (Parameters × Bytes-per-Weight) + KV Cache + ~10-20% Overhead**. At Q4_K_M (the industry-standard \"sweet spot\"), this works out to roughly **0.5–0.6 GB per billion parameters** for weights alone.\n\n#### Comprehensive LLM VRAM Table (Q4_K_M Quantization)\n\n| Model | Total Params | VRAM (Q4_K_M weights) | Recommended Total VRAM | Fits Single Consumer GPU? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Phi-4 Mini** | 3.8B | ~2.5 GB | 4–6 GB | ✅ Any GPU |\n| **Qwen3-4B** | 4B | ~2.5 GB | 4–6 GB | ✅ Any GPU |\n| **Gemma 3 4B** | 4B | ~2.2 GB | 4–6 GB | ✅ Any GPU |\n| **Llama 3.1 8B** | 8B | ~5 GB | 8–10 GB | ✅ 8GB+ |\n| **Qwen3-8B** | 8B | ~5 GB | 8–10 GB | ✅ 8GB+ |\n| **DeepSeek-R1 8B Distill** | 8B | ~5 GB | 6–8 GB | ✅ 8GB+ |\n| **Mistral 7B** | 7B | ~4.5 GB | 6–8 GB | ✅ 8GB+ |\n| **Gemma 3 12B** | 12B | ~6.7 GB | 10–12 GB | ✅ 12GB+ |\n| **Mistral Nemo 12B** | 12B | ~8 GB | 10–12 GB | ✅ 12GB+ |\n| **Phi-4 14B** | 14B | ~9 GB | 12–14 GB | ✅ 16GB+ |\n| **Qwen3-14B** | 14B | ~9 GB | 12–16 GB | ✅ 16GB+ |\n| **Qwen3-32B** | 32B | ~19.5 GB | 24–32 GB | ✅ 24GB (tight) |\n| **DeepSeek-R1 32B Distill** | 32B | ~18 GB | 18–24 GB | ✅ 24GB |\n| **Gemma 3 27B** | 27B | ~15 GB | 17–22 GB | ✅ 24GB |\n| **Mixtral 8x7B (MoE)** | 47B total | ~24 GB | 24–28 GB | ⚠️ Tight on 24GB |\n| **Llama 3.3 70B** | 70B | ~40 GB | 42–50 GB | ❌ Need 2×24GB or 48GB |\n| **DeepSeek-R1 70B Distill** | 70B | ~40 GB | 40–48 GB | ❌ Need multi-GPU |\n| **Llama 4 Scout (MoE)** | 109B total (17B active) | ~34 GB (IQ1_S) / ~66 GB (Q4) | 36–70 GB | ❌ Multi-GPU needed for Q4 |\n| **Qwen3-235B-A22B (MoE)** | 235B total (22B active) | ~117 GB (Q4) | 130+ GB | ❌ Requires massive multi-GPU |\n| **Llama 4 Maverick (MoE)** | 400B total | ~122 GB (1.78-bit) / ~200 GB (Q4) | 130–220+ GB | ❌ Datacenter-class |\n| **DeepSeek-R1 Full** | 671B | ~350 GB+ | 360+ GB | ❌ Datacenter only |\n\n**Critical MoE Note:** Mixture-of-Experts models (Llama 4, Qwen3-235B, etc.) must load ALL expert weights into memory even though only a fraction (~17-22B) are active per token. VRAM is determined by **total** parameters, not active parameters.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Image Generation Model VRAM Requirements\n\n| Model | Precision | VRAM Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Stable Diffusion 1.5** | FP16 | ~4 GB (min), 8 GB rec. | Lightweight, mature |\n| **SDXL** | FP16 | ~6-8 GB (min), 12-16 GB rec. | Standard for quality work |\n| **SD 3.5 Medium** | FP16 | ~10 GB | Good balance |\n| **SD 3.5 Large** | FP16 | ~18 GB | 8B param model |\n| **Flux.1 Dev/Schnell** | FP16 | 18–24 GB | DiT architecture, very demanding |\n| **Flux.1 Dev/Schnell** | FP8 | 13–15 GB | Good compromise |\n| **Flux.1 Dev/Schnell** | NF4/GGUF Q4 | 6–10 GB | Accessible to mid-range GPUs |\n\n**Key insight:** For modern image gen with ControlNet + LoRAs + higher resolutions, **12-16 GB is the practical floor**, and **24 GB** is strongly recommended for full-precision Flux workflows.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Voice/Audio Model VRAM Requirements\n\n#### Speech-to-Text (Whisper ASR)\n| Model Size | Parameters | VRAM |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tiny | 39M | ~1 GB |\n| Base | 74M | ~1 GB |\n| Small | 244M | ~2 GB |\n| Medium | 769M | ~5 GB |\n| Turbo | 809M | ~6 GB |\n| Large-v3 | 1.55B | ~10 GB |\n\n**Tip:** `faster-whisper` achieves 3-4x speedup over standard implementation.\n\n#### Text-to-Speech (TTS)\n| Model | VRAM | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Kokoro-82M | <2-3 GB | Fast, runs on CPU or low-VRAM GPUs |\n| Chatterbox Turbo | ~2-4 GB | Low-latency, optimized for voice agents |\n| Zonos-v0.1 | ~6 GB+ | Emotional control, voice cloning |\n| VoxCPM2 | ~6 GB+ | 48kHz output, voice cloning |\n| XTTS v2 | ~8 GB+ | Popular but outperformed by newer models |\n\n**Summary:** Voice/audio is the **least VRAM-demanding** category. 8 GB handles most use cases comfortably. Only advanced voice cloning or running full STT+TTS pipelines simultaneously alongside an LLM pushes toward 12-16 GB.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Video Generation Model VRAM Requirements\n\n| Model | Variant | VRAM (Optimized) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Wan 2.1/2.2** | 1.3B | 4–8 GB | Budget-friendly, ~4 min for 5s 480p clip |\n| **Wan 2.1/2.2** | 14B (480p) | 18–22 GB (FP8) | RTX 4090 can handle this |\n| **Wan 2.1/2.2** | 14B (720p) | 22–26 GB (FP8) | At 24GB card limit, often fails |\n| **Wan 2.1/2.2** | 14B (720p+ full) | 54–65 GB | Multi-GPU/cloud required |\n| **CogVideoX** | 2B | ~8 GB | Fast iteration, lower VRAM |\n| **CogVideoX** | 5B | 12–16 GB | Requires FP8/tiling |\n\n**Critical reality:** Video generation is the most VRAM-hungry category. Even with optimization, the 14B Wan model at 720p pushes beyond 24 GB. Generation times are long: ~12+ minutes per clip on RTX 4090. This is a frontier use case where 24 GB is barely adequate and 32+ GB is ideal.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Real-World Performance Benchmarks (Tokens/Second)\n\n#### Usability Tiers\n| Speed | User Experience |\n|---|---|\n| **<10 t/s** | Barely interactive; trigger-and-wait |\n| **10-20 t/s** | Minimum functional for chat (matches reading speed) |\n| **30-40 t/s** | Comfortable \"sweet spot\" for interactive use |\n| **60-100+ t/s** | High-performance; agentic workflows, rapid coding |\n\n#### Performance by VRAM/GPU Tier (Q4_K_M models)\n\n| VRAM Tier | Model Size Range | Typical Speed | Example GPU |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **8 GB** | 7-9B only | 30-60 t/s | RTX 4060 |\n| **12 GB** | 7-14B | 20-40 t/s | RTX 3060 12GB |\n| **16 GB** | 14-20B | 20-40 t/s | RTX 4070 Ti Super |\n| **24 GB** | 30-35B comfortably | 30-45 t/s (8B), 30-40 t/s (32B) | RTX 4090 |\n| **32 GB** | 32-46B comfortably | 60-100+ t/s (8B), varies larger | RTX 5090 |\n\n#### Specific GPU Benchmarks (2025-2026)\n| GPU | Model | Speed |\n|---|---|---|\n| RTX 4090 (24GB) | Llama 3.1 8B | ~165 t/s |\n| RTX 5090 (32GB) | Llama 3.1 8B | ~213 t/s (+29%) |\n| RTX 4090 | Qwen3 14B | ~98 t/s |\n| RTX 5090 | Qwen3 14B | ~148 t/s (+51%) |\n| RTX 4090 | Qwen3 32B (Q4) | ~45 t/s |\n| RTX 5090 | Qwen3 32B (Q4) | ~61 t/s (+36%) |\n\n**The \"bandwidth cliff\":** If a model doesn't fit entirely in VRAM and spills to system RAM, performance crashes from 30-100+ t/s down to **1-5 t/s**. This is the single most important factor — partial offloading is a massive penalty.\n\n**The formula:** `Tokens/sec ≈ Memory Bandwidth (GB/s) ÷ Model Size (GB)` (real-world is ~50-80% of theoretical).\n\n---\n\n### 6. Local Inference Tools & Their VRAM Requirements\n\n| Tool | Backend | Best For | VRAM Strategy |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **LM Studio** | llama.cpp / MLX | Beginners, GUI, A/B testing | Auto-manages; visual VRAM monitoring |\n| **Ollama** | llama.cpp | Developers, API integration | \"Docker for LLMs\"; auto-allocates |\n| **llama.cpp** | Native | Max control, low-VRAM, CPU | Granular layer offloading (-ngl); allows GPU/CPU split |\n| **vLLM** | Native | Production serving, multi-user | PagedAttention; crashes on OOM (no spill to RAM) |\n| **ComfyUI** | PyTorch | Image/video generation | Dynamic offloading; best for Flux/SD/Wan |\n\n**Key insight:** Ollama and LM Studio are thin wrappers around llama.cpp — performance is nearly identical when configured the same way. The tool choice doesn't significantly affect VRAM requirements for single-user use; hardware is the bottleneck.\n\n**Embedding models for RAG:** Extremely lightweight. nomic-embed-text needs ~0.3-0.5 GB; all-MiniLM needs ~0.1-0.3 GB. These are negligible alongside any LLM.\n\n---\n\n## Evidence Table\n\n| Source | Key Claim | Tier |\n|---|---|---|\n| willitrunai.com | Llama 3.3 70B Q4_K_M needs ~40-44 GB for weights | Tier B (verified tool) |\n| unsloth.ai | Llama 4 Scout 109B at IQ1_S needs ~34 GB VRAM | Tier A (model publisher) |\n| stability.ai | SD 3.5 Medium requires ~9.9 GB FP16 | Tier A (official) |\n| localllm.in | 8 GB VRAM fits 7-9B models; 24 GB fits 30-35B at Q4 | Tier B (community reference) |\n| HuggingFace GGUF Calculator | Community tool for calculating VRAM from model architecture | Tier A (verified tool) |\n| Reddit/community consensus | 10-20 t/s is minimum usable; <10 t/s feels sluggish | Tier B (verified by multiple sources) |\n| Multiple benchmarks | RTX 4090: ~165 t/s on 8B; ~45 t/s on 32B Q4 | Tier B (aggregated benchmarks) |\n| Multiple benchmarks | RTX 5090: 29-51% faster than 4090 due to bandwidth | Tier B (multiple sources agree) |\n| Community consensus | CPU offloading drops speed to 1-5 t/s | Tier B (widely confirmed) |\n\n---\n\n## Counter-Evidence Considered\n\n1. **\"Small models are good enough\":** SLMs (1B-4B) are genuinely impressive in 2025-2026 for targeted tasks (summarization, classification, simple coding). A 3-4B model on 4-8 GB VRAM is usable. **However**, these models are not competitive with 14B-32B models for complex reasoning, multi-step coding, or agentic workflows. The counter-argument has merit for casual users but not for power users wanting frontier local AI.\n\n2. **\"Apple Silicon solves the VRAM problem\":** Unified Memory (up to 128 GB on M4 Max) allows running 70B+ models locally. **However**, Apple Silicon has lower memory bandwidth than discrete GPUs (~546 GB/s M4 Max vs. ~1,792 GB/s RTX 5090), so tokens/sec is slower per unit of compute. It's a valid alternative path but doesn't eliminate the need for large memory — it just provides it differently.\n\n3. **\"Quantization makes VRAM irrelevant\":** Aggressive quantization (1-2 bit) can dramatically shrink models. **However**, below Q4 (4-bit), model quality degrades noticeably. There's a floor below which you lose too much capability. Q4_K_M remains the community standard because lower is visibly worse.\n\n4. **\"CPU offloading makes any model runnable on any hardware\":** Technically true, but practically false for interactive use. Offloading to RAM drops speeds to 1-5 t/s, which is barely tolerable for any interactive use.\n\n---\n\n## Gaps\n\n1. **Exact laptop GPU benchmarks:** Most benchmarks focus on desktop GPUs (RTX 4090/5090). Laptop GPU benchmarks (RTX 4060/4070 Laptop, which have lower power/bandwidth) are less documented but would show ~30-50% lower performance than desktop equivalents.\n\n2. **Combined workload VRAM:** Running an LLM + embedding model + image generation simultaneously is a real use case but poorly benchmarked. In practice, you'd need to swap models in/out of VRAM or have enough to hold multiple models.\n\n3. **Fine-tuning VRAM requirements:** Local fine-tuning/LoRA training was out of scope but typically requires 2-3x the inference VRAM due to optimizer states and gradients. This is almost impractical below 24 GB for useful model sizes.\n\n4. **Specific 2026 laptop GPU lineups:** The exact laptop GPU options available in September 2026 (RTX 50-series laptop variants, potential 16GB/24GB laptop GPUs) would affect the recommendation but depend on hardware research.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Recommendation Matrix\n\n| VRAM | What You Can Run Comfortably | Limitation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **8 GB** | 7-8B LLMs, SD 1.5/SDXL, Whisper Small, basic TTS | Can't run 14B+ models; limited context; no Flux/SD3 at full quality |\n| **12 GB** | 12-14B LLMs, SDXL, Flux Q4, Whisper Large, Wan 1.3B video | 27B+ models need offloading (slow); limited Flux quality |\n| **16 GB** | 14-20B LLMs, Flux FP8, good context windows | 27B+ tight; no 32B without offloading |\n| **24 GB** | 27-35B LLMs, Flux FP16, Wan 14B 480p, all voice models | 70B+ impossible on single card; video gen limited at 720p+ |\n| **32 GB** | 32-46B LLMs comfortably, all image gen, video gen at higher quality | 70B still needs quantization; MoE models (100B+) still too large |\n","Recipient":"c9e10e04-5086-4628-b678-26c6a5b93a9a","toolAction":"Sending comprehensive findings","toolSummary":"Research findings report"}}]}
